**Resubmitted: T4C — deviation (high), preferred level.**

Repair aimed at the "Customer convenience termination present" check: the operative grant now names the customer role as the subject of the right, so the sentence reads **"Corio is the customer under this Agreement, and the customer may terminate this Agreement for convenience upon thirty (30) days' prior written notice to Commerce One"** — the customer-side convenience right is stated in a single contiguous operative phrase rather than inferred from the party name or a parenthetical. Lowercase "customer" avoids any collision with the Section 1.6 defined term CUSTOMER(S) (Corio's own sublicensees).

Section 11.4 remains complete at the preferred level in one insertion:
1. Customer (Corio) convenience termination right;
2. 30 days' prior written notice;
3. Commerce One refunds prepaid fees pro rata for the post-termination period;
4. Express bar on Commerce One convenience termination during the initial five-year term or any renewal term.

Sections 11.1–11.3 are retained verbatim, including Corio's post-termination Customer access rights, the surviving support obligation, and the mutual breach/insolvency exits. No "in whole or in part", survival tail, expense allocation, or procedural extras were added, since no selected element requires them.
